    "title": "Somali Parliament Speaker Accuses Neighboring Countries of Colluding with Al-Shabaab",
    "original_title": "Somalia parliament speaker accuses neighbours of colluding with Al-Shabaab",
    "slug": "somali-parliament-speaker-accuses-neighboring-countries-of-colluding-with-al-shabaab",
    "summary": "Speaker Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur alleged that Kenya, Ethiopia, and Uganda are colluding with Al-Shabaab and that Israel has united these countries with militants against the federal government.",
    "content": "MOGADISHU, Somalia — The Speaker of Somalia's parliament, Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur, has publicly accused Kenya, Ethiopia, and Uganda of colluding with the Islamist militant group Al-Shabaab. In a speech delivered in Mogadishu, Nur claimed that Israel has brought together African Union troop-contributing countries, Al-Shabaab, and forces loyal to a former regional leader to form a united front against the federal government. He did not provide specific evidence to support these allegations.\n\nNur further alleged that unnamed foreign powers are attempting to colonize Somalia and urged Somalis to resist these efforts. He described the security architecture supporting the federal government as compromised, suggesting that external actors have infiltrated key institutions. The speech comes amid heightened political tensions in Somalia, as the government prepares for elections and continues its fight against Al-Shabaab.\n\nKenya, Ethiopia, and Uganda are among the countries that contribute troops to the African Union Mission in Somalia (AMISOM), which has been fighting Al-Shabaab since 2007. These nations have been key allies in Somalia's security efforts, and the accusation of collusion marks a significant shift in rhetoric. The speaker's claims have not been independently verified, and there has been no immediate response from the governments of Kenya, Ethiopia, or Uganda.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these allegations are particularly concerning because they involve countries that host large Somali refugee populations and have been involved in Somalia's stabilization efforts. Many diaspora members have family ties to these neighboring nations and rely on them for trade, travel, and remittances. If the accusations escalate into diplomatic tensions, it could disrupt these vital connections and further complicate the diaspora's relationship with both Somalia and its neighbors.\n\nThis development also raises questions about the unity of the international coalition supporting Somalia. The African Union mission has been a cornerstone of security in the region, and any perception of collusion could undermine trust and cooperation. The speaker's call for Somalis to rise against foreign powers may resonate with diaspora communities who are sensitive to issues of sovereignty and external interference, potentially fueling nationalist sentiments.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-speaker-accuses-neighbours-colluding-al-shabaab/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-speaker-accuses-neighbours-colluding-al-shabaab/",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Politics",
    "published_at": "2026-11-24T10:05:14.000Z",
    "first_seen_at": "2026-11-24T19:48:05.566Z",
