    "title": "Fighting in Barawe District Displaces Hundreds of Families in Lower Shabelle",
    "original_title": "Conflict in Barawe district, Lower Shabelle, sends families fleeing for safety",
    "slug": "fighting-in-barawe-district-displaces-hundreds-of-families-in-lower-shabelle",
    "summary": "Clashes between Somali government forces and Al-Shabaab in Barawe district have forced hundreds of families to flee to Kurtunwarey, where they are living without adequate shelter, food, or water.",
    "content": "Hundreds of families have been displaced from Barawe district in Somalia's Lower Shabelle region following renewed fighting between Somali government forces and the militant group Al-Shabaab. The displaced families have sought refuge in areas of Kurtunwarey district, where they are reportedly living in the open with insufficient access to food and water.\n\nAmong those affected is Ahmed Omar, who has been sheltering his family of nine under trees for the past month. The family, which includes his wife, six children, and an elderly man, relies on the generosity of local host families for food. According to Omar, the support they receive amounts to about one meal a day, which is not enough to meet their needs.\n\nThe clashes in Barawe are part of a broader pattern of conflict in Lower Shabelle, where government forces and Al-Shabaab have vied for control over territory for years. Barawe was formerly a key stronghold for Al-Shabaab before being recaptured by government forces in 2014, but the group has continued to carry out attacks and maintain a presence in rural areas.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the displacement crisis in Lower Shabelle is a reminder of the ongoing instability in the region and the humanitarian needs it creates. Many diaspora members have family ties to the affected areas and often mobilize to send remittances and support relief efforts. The displacement also underscores the challenges of delivering aid in conflict zones, where access is frequently constrained.\n\nLocal authorities and aid agencies have yet to provide a comprehensive response to the latest displacement, and the number of affected families is expected to rise as fighting continues. The displaced families in Kurtunwarey are in urgent need of shelter, food, water, and medical assistance.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/11/conflict-in-barawe-district-lower-shabelle-sends-families-fleeing-for-safety/",
